{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed218530468/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"50c07299\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nMove the diagonal foreground segment to the next longer run along the same up-right diagonal.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color. The foreground pixels form a\ncontiguous one-pixel-wide segment on a single anti-diagonal.\n\nRule\nErase the current segment. If its columns are c0..c1 and its length is L, draw the same\ncolor on columns c1+1..c1+L+1 along the same anti-diagonal.\n\nKey insight\nThe segment advances past its current upper-right end by one whole segment position, and\nthe next segment is one pixel longer than the current one.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7acdf6d3\" lines=\"25-42\">\n<description>\nMove the source-color pixels into the unique outline interior whose size matches the source count.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color forms 8-connected outline objects with row-wise interior cells.\n- The other foreground color supplies a set of source pixels away from the chosen outline.\nRule\n- For each possible outline color, compute each 8-connected outline object's\n  row-span interior: background pixels strictly between the leftmost and\n  rightmost outline pixels on each occupied row.\n- Select the unique outline interior whose cell count equals the total\n  number of pixels of the other foreground color.\n- Erase the source pixels and paint that matched interior with the source color.\nKey insight\n- The colors are roles, not fixed IDs: the source color is identified by\n  counting, and the destination is the outline with a matching interior area.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f0f8a26d\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nRotate every foreground bar 90 degrees around its own center on the same canvas.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color, and all foreground pixels share one color.\nEach 4-connected foreground object is a solid one-cell-thick horizontal or vertical\nbar with odd length, with single-pixel bars allowed.\n\nRule\nClear the canvas to the background, then redraw every bar with its midpoint, length,\nand color preserved while swapping horizontal and vertical orientation.\n\nKey insight\nThe bars are independent objects: their centers and lengths identify the output\nplacement, so no matching, ordering, or global symmetry is needed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9385bd28\" lines=\"71-87\">\n<description>\nFill rectangles between diagonal marker pairs using a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with a vertical legend at the bottom-left: each row is a (marker_color, fill_color) pair.\n- Scattered L-shaped corner markers of each marker color define opposite corners of axis-aligned rectangles.\n- Some markers are solid 2×2 blocks instead of corner pairs.\n\nRule:\n- Read legend bottom-to-top; for each entry find all pixels of that marker color.\n- Determine which two bbox corners carry L-shaped anchors — the *other* diagonal defines the fill region.\n- Fill the diamond (all shortest-path cells between those two corners) with the legend's fill color.\n- Solid 2×2 marker blocks are simply recolored to the fill color.\n- Later legend entries are protected from earlier fills.\n\nKey insight: The L-shaped anchors sit on the marker diagonal; the fill goes on the opposite diagonal of the bounding box.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"71e489b6\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nHighlight black defects in blue foreground by surrounding them with orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue (1) foreground on black (0) bg; small black holes or protrusions within blue regions; occasional thin blue spurs into black areas.\n\nRule:\n- Thin blue pixels mostly surrounded by black are absorbed into the background.\n- Small black components (≤3 cells) and leaf pixels of larger black components are \"defects.\"\n- Each defect gets an orange (7) 8-neighbor halo; the defect pixels themselves stay black.\n\nKey insight: Only structural anomalies—tiny isolated patches and dangling endpoints—are marked, not bulk black regions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"99caaf76\" lines=\"56-75\">\n<description>\nMove each marked cross object away from its marker to the grid edge while rotating the colored cross 180 degrees.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each 8-connected foreground object has nine pixels: a 4-pixel marker-color\n  sprite attached to a 5-pixel non-marker colored cross.\n- Each object bbox is either 3x6 or 6x3, so the marker sprite is on exactly\n  one side of the cross.\n\nRule:\n- Move the whole object straight away from its marker side until the object\n  bbox touches the corresponding grid edge.\n- Keep the marker sprite unchanged within the object.\n- Rotate the 3x3 colored cross block by 180 degrees before placing it.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker sprite is a directional tail; the cross is the leading payload,\nand only that payload turns around during the slide to the edge.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d35bdbdc\" lines=\"120-134\">\n<description>\nTransfer center colors between bordered sprites matched by color, keeping only path-connected ones.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, grey (5) path network, and several small \"bordered sprites\" each having a border color and a single center pixel of a different color.\n- Grey path forms a one-stroke trail under 8-connectivity; its start/end cells touch exactly two destination sprites via ports.\n\nRule:\n- Destination sprites are determined by the grey trail's two endpoints.\n- For each destination, find the source sprite whose border color equals the destination's center color.\n- Replace the destination's center pixel with the source's center color.\n- Delete all non-destination sprites; grey paths and destinations remain.\n\nKey insight: Center color of each destination acts as a lookup key into the border colors of other sprites, enabling indirect color transfer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2ba387bc\" lines=\"6-21\">\n<description>\nSort scattered 4×4 squares into two columns by type: frames left, solids right.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Multiple 4×4 objects on bg.\n- Frames: colored 1px border, bg-filled 2×2 interior.\n- Solids: entirely filled with one non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Classify each 4×4 object as frame or solid by its interior.\n- Sort each group top-to-bottom by input row.\n- Stack frames in left column (cols 0–3), solids in right (cols 4–7).\n- Output is 8 wide; height = 4 × max(#frames, #solids), bg-padded.\n\nKey insight: Frame vs solid is determined solely by whether the 2×2 interior matches bg.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9aaea919\" lines=\"38-51\">\n<description>\nTransfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.\n- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.\n- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.\n- All marker bars are removed.\n\nKey insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3d588dc9\" lines=\"95-109\">\n<description>\nReplace the active black cutout's tail with background and mark the cutout side facing a colored source region.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
